from 1980 to 2008 Oklahoma averaged  fewer than two earthquakes a year last  year they were 585 this year they're on  pace for 870 that makes Oklahoma the  most seismically active state in the  continental US surpassing even  California so what's going on under  Oklahoma turns out there are thousands  of fault lines running under the state  and until recently those fault lines had  been pretty quiet now geologists are  worried that huge increases in fracking  and the disposal of fracking wastewater  near those fault lines might be causing  the earthquakes advanced fracking  technology allows companies to drill  horizontally through shale rock  formations a high pressure mix of water  sand and chemicals injected down the  well creates tiny fractures in the rock  forcing oil and gas reserves back to the  surface  but vast quantities of toxic saltwater  that's been trapped underground for  millions of years is also unlocked at  the surface the oil and gas are  separated from the water and sold to  market but the wastewater has to go  somewhere in Oklahoma almost all of it  gets put down wastewater disposal wells  which inject the water deep underground  into what's called the Arbuckle  formation a thick porous layer of  limestone running under the state that  absorbs water easily and in theory is a  good place to trap all that waste water  but remember all those fault lines  seismologists believe the huge infusion  of water over the last several years has  lubricated the fault lines causing them  to slip and creating more earthquakes  than Oklahoma has ever felt
 

